A||||15||fiero56||My thoughts on Poly bushings||||14||
Z||000000||fiero56||02-09-2000||03:55 PM||||While driving up a ramp at the parking garage, I finally snapped one of the connecting link bolts for the front sway bar. I drove it home, went to pep boys, and they were out of the stock end link kit, but did have a poly-urethane set. I reluectantly bought them(I was in a bind), thinking they would be too harsh of a ride. Not so!!! They work great! There is no longer any play in the front end while pulling into the drive, bumps are very smooth and there is no more steering wheel j-e-r-k(have to do it this way or it gets starred out) while going over bumps. Even though the Fiero cornered very well before, it does it even better. I love it, think I'll do the rear bar too.||||reg||14
Z||000001||GREENDANO||02-09-2000||06:10 PM||||While you're at it get a set of urethane frame bushings for the bar too.  That will really make the sway bar work.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||14
Z||000002||fiero56||02-09-2000||07:41 PM||||When I broke the bolt, I drove the car home, disconnected the sway bar and drove the car to Pep Boys. The whole way there and back, the nose would dive a little when stopping, and would lean quite a bit for a Fiero when cornering. I'm pretty sure the new poly urethan bushings have hepled a lot. The stock mounts only had 15,000 miles. I love this setup. The ride is so much smoother, no more jerky steering wheel, and the car sticks to the road on bumpy pavement even better than before. Plus it looks really cool with the red bushings back there.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric||02-09-2000||09:57 PM||||Weren't your stock end links poly also?  On my car they are kind of a yellowish color.  I don't doubt the new ones work better, but I really think you had polys to start with.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||02-09-2000||10:06 PM||||They might be. I was assuming the "orange" ones were a slightly different material. The red ones I put on, do not compress at all. They hold their shape. The orange ones have these flat spots on the sides. I'm sure the new bolts play their part too.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2FAST4U||02-09-2000||11:18 PM||||Since you guy's are talking about poly bushings I thought I might add a some advice that I learned today while talking to the guys at ProThane.<BR>I'e herd several guy's complain in previious threads about squeaking poly bushings.<BR>The way to avoid squeeks is to leave all mounting bolts loose after installation. set the car on the ground to load the bushings, then torque everything to proper specs while the suspension is loaded. To gain clearance to get under the car drive it up on ramps or use an alignment rack.<P>2FAST4U||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||02-10-2000||10:14 AM||||I had also remembered the squeaking bushing complaints. I've also heard some powered graphite will take care of it. I thinking it won't squeak(or as much) since these bushings are just on the stabilizer bar, and don't see the amount of movement of bushings go through.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Racer 1||02-10-2000||11:24 AM||||My biggest mostake was putting them poly bushings on all my suspension arms. The ride really sucks and squeeks real loud but the handling kicks azz so if you have a bad back dont get poly. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||02-10-2000||01:15 PM||||I wouldn't consider putting polys all around on everything. Just the stabilizer bars, maybe a few other "little" places, but not on the suspension arms or similar. My Fiero rides stiff enough for me, I don't need it any tighter, or making any squeaks. <BR>The end links for the sway bar were in sad shape. I wasn't able to unbolt them, they just twisted into pieces when a wrench was put on them. I had to dremel off the washers. I am amazed at how much work the front sway bar acutally does for the suspension overall. Not just to prevent body roll, but the whole front end is much tighter and more responsive.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||02-10-2000||09:08 PM||||I used my dremel to put grooves in my poly sway bar bushings, and drilled/tapped them to accept grease fittings.  Made a big difference.  Summit sells them pre-made.  My next project is to do the same thing with the control arm bushings.  For now, I just use a syringe to squirt a little graphite/alcohol suspension around the control arm bushings.  It keeps the squeaking down a bit.  I may try selectively replacing the poly pieces with the stock rubber bushings, to try to reduce the ride harshness.  Anybody else ever try this?<BR>On a side note, my rear tires wear nice and even with the poly control arm bushings.  I used to be down to the cords on the inside edge after only 20k miles, but I have 40k now, nice and even.  <p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 02-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||02-11-2000||05:22 PM||||I really like the poly bushings on my Batmobile. Admittedly, it's not a daily driver and the old bushings were mush, but I think it rides great. Stiff, but not too harsh. I bought the graphite impregnated ones for the sway bar, so hopefully they won't ever squeak much. On the control arm bushings, I tapped some threads into the inside of the bolt holes and used the grease that came with the bushings. The grooves hold the grease and help lubricate the bushing.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eddie.H||02-11-2000||05:32 PM||||I drive mine to mid town some times and I am thinking of buying a kidney belt.||||reg||6
Z||000012||2birds||02-11-2000||11:06 PM||||For some unexplained reason, they haven't perfected the fine art of installing a railroad crossing down here, and I am reminded of this constantly by mine. ||||reg||9
Z||000013||lowCG||02-12-2000||06:23 PM||||Unless you have racing tires and a cage,I don't think that the front poly bushings are going to do a whole lot,other than transmit every cigarette butt that you pass over right to the seat of your pants.<BR>The rear bushings help to get the power to the ground,and,since that's where the weight is,the firmness is less of a problem.<BR>Mine don't squeak because I put a groove,filled with grease inside of the bushing.||||reg||1
Z||000014||mshill||02-12-2000||09:44 PM||||My car was making all kinds of noise and had a ton of slop in the steering until I put poly bushings on the front a-arms and sway bar.  I also added poly bushings to the dogbone but didn't notice much difference there.  <P>The backend still seemed to wander a little at high speeds (over 70) but that will be fixed after I get the rear a-arm and cradle bushings are poly as well.<P>Sure the ride is a little harsher, but I would rather have it that way than wandering all over the road every time I go over a little bump or ridge in the road.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Eric||02-13-2000||11:55 AM||||LowCG, could you describe the groove you made in the bushing?  Was there just one?  Which direction did it go relative to the front of the car and how deep was it?||||reg||1
